                      CHAPTER EIGHTEEN


                            The Plan
                     T R AC K   “Lean on Me” — Bill Withers
                        ECHO      the café back room, 1944




The pass came by messenger on Monday, in a cream envelope with the
iris in the corner.
    Inside: a lanyard, a map of the loading route, and a sticky note in a
small neat hand. Neighbor. I’m so glad. I laid it on the café counter in
front of everyone and said, “This is the plan.”
    “That’s a badge,” said Sofía.
    “It’s how we get in. We walk through the front with their pass, car‐
rying the thing they want us to carry, and we lie about nothing. They
already expect everything. A hidden thing is just one more story we’d
have to keep straight.”
    Mama Tete, at the end of the table in her sunglasses, said, “Finally.”
    We had five days.
    Sofía had drafted a plan four single-spaced pages long, complete
with a key. Mama Tete took possession of the pages, folded them into
quarters, used them as a cushion, and asked who was in charge of wa‐
ter. After that, the meeting had a sensible size.
    I grabbed a marker and an empty carton off the supply shelf and
wrote FIRST AID across its side, and the ink soaked through to the
napkins inside, and I reached out to steady myself on the table.
    The back room, 1944.




